## 5. Where does obstruction-free fit?

There are three commonly discussed non-blocking progress guarantees:

| Guarantee        | Minimum condition for progress                                      |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Obstruction-free | A thread completes if it eventually runs alone without interference |
| Lock-free        | The system keeps completing operations under contention             |
| Wait-free        | Every thread completes in a bounded number of its own steps         |

Their guarantees become progressively stronger:

\\[ \text{Wait-free} \Rightarrow \text{Lock-free} \Rightarrow \text{Obstruction-free} \\]

The reverse implications do not generally hold.

## 6. What about C++ atomics?

A crucial distinction for C++ interviews: using `std::atomic` does not automatically make an algorithm lock-free or wait-free.

For example:

```
std::atomic<int> counter{0};

counter.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
```

This performs an atomic increment, but the C++ standard does not guarantee that every atomic type is implemented lock-free on every platform. You can check the implementation with:

```
bool is_lock_free = counter.is_lock_free();
```

Even if `fetch_add()` is lock-free on your platform, that does not automatically make your entire algorithm wait-free. Algorithm-level progress guarantees must be analyzed separately.

Also, wait-free means a bounded number of algorithmic steps, not a guaranteed wall-clock completion time. An operating system could deschedule a thread, and hardware delays can 
still affect elapsed time.

## 7. When should you use each?

- Lock-free: Concurrent queues, stacks, counters, and other high-contention structures where system-wide progress and avoiding blocking are important.
- Wait-free: Real-time or latency-sensitive systems where every participating thread needs a bounded algorithmic completion guarantee, assuming the required execution model.
- Mutex-based synchronization: Often the simplest and most maintainable option when non-blocking progress is not a strict requirement.

For your C++ multithreading and concurrency interview preparation, remember this one-line answer:

> Lock-free prevents the entire system from getting stuck; wait-free prevents any individual thread from being perpetually denied progress by the algorithm.
